Q: Is fixing child.prototype.constructor necessary in ES5 if we solely rely on [[Prototype]] check methods like Object.getPrototypeOf()? A: <p>To answer the question as literally asked, upfront: omitting the <code>constructor</code> correction will never cause a native error. No runtime mechanism reads this property ... not <code>new</code>, not method dispatch, and not <code>instanceof</code>, which compares against the function's <code>prototype</code> property while walking the <code>[[Prototype]]</code> chain. The risk is entirely contractual. After <code>Child.prototype = Object.create(Parent.prototype)</code> the property does not go missing, it just answers wrongly ... <code>instance.constructor</code> resolves, one link up the chain, to the parent constructor. But one could argue that a property that answers wrongly is worse than one that is absent.</p>
<p>As for relying on <code>instanceof</code> and <code>Object.getPrototypeOf</code> as the single source of truth ... one does not even need a cross-realm type-detection scenario in order to demonstrate that <code>instanceof</code> is not entirely reliable. Since ES2015 its behavior is customizable via <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Symbol/hasInstance" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Symbol.hasInstance</code></a>, and for plain constructor functions the <code>prototype</code> property itself is writable. Thus the supposedly tamper-proof native check is itself just two mutable reads away from being spoofed.</p>
<p>The aforementioned cross-realm type-detection scenario then finishes the argument. For foreign-realm instances the <code>instanceof</code> operator is of no use at all (identity-based, wrong realm's constructors), and everything that relies on <code>Object.prototype.toString.call(value)</code> can be spoofed via <code>Symbol.toStringTag</code>.</p>
<p>But one always can make use of <code>Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject)</code>, and this prototype-object happens to have OWN property-descriptors. And one of them refers to the constructor; and the latter has own property-descriptors as well. And one of them refers to the constructor's name.</p>
<p>Thus a throw-safe query (yes, direct property access like <code>value.constructor</code> or <code>functionType.name</code> can be spoofed/hijacked as well, e.g. by property-accessor proxy-traps) for a given value's type via its prototype and constructor-name looks like this ...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject) ?? Object.create(null),
    'constructor',
  )?.value ?? Object.create(null),
  'name',
)?.value;
</code></pre>
<p>This is just one arm, but the fastest, for checking on a foreign-realm object's type. The second arm is a full structural check on the prototype-chain and the validation of the correctly implemented (behavioral) contract/s of a known and/or expected type. And notice what the fast arm reads: exactly the <code>constructor</code> reference the OP considers omitting. The back-reference is not a class-nostalgic cosmetic; it is the data a realm-independent type-check depends on.</p>
<p>There is a reason why one should follow the path of providing an honest prototype-chain. And honesty extends to the descriptor kind. The widely taught correction ...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>childSide.prototype.constructor = childSide;
</code></pre>
<p>... assigns the correct value but creates an <em>enumerable</em> own property, whereas the specification's shape - and what <code>class</code> syntax produces - is <code>{ writable: true, enumerable: false, configurable: true }</code>. The faithful repair therefore establishes the property together with the prototype-object itself ...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function inheritPrototype(childSide, parentSide) {
  childSide.prototype = Object.create(parentSide.prototype, {
    constructor: {
      value: childSide,
      writable: true,
      enumerable: false,
      configurable: true,
    },
  });
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>Animal</code>/<code>Dog</code> example by @Davi demonstrates the importance. If one converts this very example-code into class-syntax based code, one gets the correct constructor-references within the prototype-chain for free ...</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const hazel = new Dog('Hazel');
const buddy = new Dog('Buddy');

console.log({
  hazel: {
    name: hazel.name,
    species: hazel.species,
  },
  buddy: {
    name: buddy.name,
    species: buddy.species,
  },
});
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(hazel),
  ),
);
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(
      Object.getPrototypeOf(buddy),
    ),
  ),
);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.as-console-wrapper { bottom: auto; right: auto; top: 0; min-height: 100%; }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  class Animal {
    #species;

    constructor(species) {
      this.#species = species;
    }
    get species() {
      return this.#species;
    }
  }

  class Dog extends Animal {
    #name;

    constructor(name) {
      super('Dog');

      this.#name = name;
    }
    get name() {
      return this.#name;
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to avoid extent report to not to overwrite the html file name A: <p>I also faced a similar issue. As in the real-world, we need old reports as well. Below is the solution in Java for Extent PDF report</p>
<p>I added an event listener method. Event used- TestRunStarted. We further need to register for this event too. The solution can be done for HTML report too.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public void setCustomReportName(TestRunStarted event) 
{
    final SimpleDateFormat sdf = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy.MM.dd.HH.mm.ss&quot;);
    Timestamp timestamp = new Timestamp(System.currentTimeMillis());
    String currenttimestamp =sdf.format(timestamp);

    Properties prop=new Properties();
    //extent.reporter.pdf.out is the name of property which tell the report path
    prop.setProperty(&quot;extent.reporter.pdf.out&quot;, &quot;test output/PdfReport/ExtentPdf_&quot;+currenttimestamp+&quot;.pdf&quot;);
    ExtentService e1 =new ExtentService();

    //ExtentReportsLoader is the inner class of ExtentService and initPdf is its private method which takes the path for report 
    Class&lt;?&gt;[] a=e1.getClass().getDeclaredClasses();

    Method met;
    //Even there is exception test run wont fail and report will also be generated (ExtentPdf.pdf)
    try {
       met = a[0].getDeclaredMethod(&quot;initPdf&quot;, Properties.class);
       met.setAccessible(true);
       met.invoke(a[0], prop);
    } catch (NoSuchMethodException e) {
       System.out.println(&quot;There is no method with name initPdf&quot;);
    } catch (SecurityException e) {
       e.printStackTrace();
    } catch (IllegalAccessException e) {
       e.printStackTrace();
    } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
       System.out.println(&quot;Argument passed to method initPdf are not correct&quot;);
    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
       e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Print a python output to an html file A: <p>With each iteration, your loop is creating new <code>&lt;head&gt;&lt;/head&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;&lt;html&gt;</code>. </p>

<p>Also, if you want to remove space between paragraphs, use <code>&lt;style&gt;p { margin: 0, !important; } &lt;/style&gt;</code>.</p>

<p>You could try this function which gives the <code>HTML</code> output you wanted.</p>

<pre><code>def printTohtml(Alist, htmlfile):    
    html =  "&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;p { margin: 0 !important; }&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n"

    title = "Study - User - zip file -  Last date modified"
    html += '\n&lt;p&gt;' + title + '&lt;/p&gt;\n'

    for line in Alist:
        para = '&lt;p&gt;' + ', '.join(line) + '&lt;/p&gt;\n'
        html += para

    with open(htmlfile, 'w') as f:
        f.write(html + "\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;")

Alist =  [['123', 'user1', 'New Compressed (zipped) Folder.zip', '05-24-17'],
['123', 'user2', 'Iam.zip', '05-19-17'], ['abcd', 'Letsee.zip', '05-22-17'],
['Here', 'whichTwo.zip', '06-01-17']]

printTohtml(Alist, 'zip_files.html')
</code></pre>

<p>The markup written to <code>zip_files.html</code> is:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;style&gt;p { margin: 0 !important; }&lt;/style&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Study - User - zip file -  Last date modified&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;123, user1, New Compressed (zipped) Folder.zip, 05-24-17&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;123, user2, Iam.zip, 05-19-17&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;abcd, Letsee.zip, 05-22-17&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here, whichTwo.zip, 06-01-17&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>The page displays the following output:</p>

<pre><code>Study - User - zip file - Last date modified    
123, user1, New Compressed (zipped) Folder.zip, 05-24-17    
123, user2, Iam.zip, 05-19-17    
abcd, Letsee.zip, 05-22-17    
Here, whichTwo.zip, 06-01-17
</code></pre>

Q: How to Routes URL Dynamic with Switch Case? A: <p>I tried to use a <code>switch</code> case for basic routing.<br />
Here is how I structured my simple MVC setup:</p>
<p>1. Entry Point (<strong><code>index.php</code>)</strong></p>
<p>This file simply loads the router.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require 'router.php';
</code></pre>
<p>2. The Router (<strong><code>router.php</code>)</strong></p>
<p>Here, we start the session, load the database configuration, and route the user based on the <code>view</code> URL parameter.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
session_start();
require 'config.php'; // Ensures $pdo is available in controllers

$view = $_GET['view'] ?? 'register'; // Default view is 'register'

switch ($view) {
    case 'register':
        require 'controllers/admin_register_controller.php';
        break;

    case 'login':
        require 'controllers/login_controller.php';
        break;

    case 'logout':
        require 'controllers/logout_controller.php';
        break;

    default:
        http_response_code(404);
        echo &quot;Page not found.&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>3. Database Configuration (<strong><code>config.php</code>)</strong></p>
<p>Simply add your database connection to <code>config.php</code>. (Alternatively, you could keep credentials here and define the PDO connection in a separate <code>database.php</code>).</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
$host = 'localhost';
$db   = 'user';
$user = 'root';
$pass = '';
$charset = 'utf8mb4';

$dsn = &quot;mysql:host=$host;dbname=$db;charset=$charset&quot;;

$options = [
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE            =&gt; PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION, 
    PDO::ATTR_DEFAULT_FETCH_MODE =&gt; PDO::FETCH_ASSOC,        
    PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES   =&gt; false,                   
];

try {
    $pdo = new PDO($dsn, $user, $pass, $options);
} catch (\PDOException $e) {
    die('Database connection failed: ' . $e-&gt;getMessage());
}
</code></pre>
<p>4. The Controller (<strong><code>controllers/admin_register_controller.php</code>)</strong></p>
<p>Handles the business logic and user validation before loading the view.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require 'database.php'; // Provides the $pdo connection
$error = '';

// Admin-only page
if (!isset($_SESSION['role']) || (int)$_SESSION['role'] !== 1) {
    die(&quot;Access denied. Admin rights required.&quot;);
}

if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    $username = trim($_POST['username'] ?? '');
    $email    = trim($_POST['email'] ?? '');
    $password = $_POST['password'] ?? '';
    $role     = isset($_POST['is_admin']) ? 1 : 0;

    if ($username === '' || $email === '' || $password === '') {
        $error = 'Please fill in all fields.';
    } elseif (!preg_match(&quot;/^[a-zA-Z0-9-' ]*$/&quot;, $username)) {
        $error = 'Username contains invalid characters.';
    } elseif (!filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {
        $error = 'Please enter a valid email address.';
    } else {
        $stmt = $pdo-&gt;prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE email = ? OR username = ?');
        $stmt-&gt;execute([$email, $username]);

        if ($stmt-&gt;fetchColumn() &gt; 0) {
            $error = 'Username or email address is already taken.';
        } else {
            $hash = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);

            $stmt = $pdo-&gt;prepare('INSERT INTO users (username, email, password, role) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)');
            if (!$stmt-&gt;execute([$username, $email, $hash, $role])) {
                $error = 'A database error occurred during registration.';
            }
        }
    }
}

// Fetch all users sorted by newest registration
$users = $pdo-&gt;query(&quot;SELECT id, username, role FROM users ORDER BY id DESC&quot;)-&gt;fetchAll();

// Render the view
require 'views/admin_register_view.php';
</code></pre>
<p>5. The View (<strong><code>views/admin_register_view.php</code>)</strong></p>
<p>A clean HTML form that displays errors if there are any.<br />
(I also used header.php and footer.php)</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php require 'includes/header.php'; ?&gt;

&lt;?php if ($error): ?&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;alert alert-danger&quot;&gt;&lt;?= htmlspecialchars($error) ?&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;?php endif; ?&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Neuen Benutzer anlegen&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;form method=&quot;post&quot; style=&quot;max-width:400px;&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;mb-2&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label&gt;Benutzername&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;username&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; required&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;mb-2&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label&gt;E-Mail&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; required&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;mb-2&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label&gt;Passwort&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; required&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;form-check mb-3&quot;&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;is_admin&quot; class=&quot;form-check-input&quot; id=&quot;is_admin&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label class=&quot;form-check-label&quot; for=&quot;is_admin&quot;&gt;Ist Admin&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;create_user&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-primary&quot;&gt;Benutzer anlegen&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

&lt;h3 class=&quot;mt-4&quot;&gt;Alle Benutzer&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;table&quot;&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Benutzername&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Rolle&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;?php foreach ($users as $u): ?&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;?= htmlspecialchars($u['username']) ?&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;?= $u['role'] == 1 ? 'Admin' : 'Kunde' ?&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;?php endforeach; ?&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;?php require 'includes/footer.php'; ?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>6. The Login (<strong><code>controllers/login_controller.php</code>)</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require 'database.php'; // Provides the $pdo connection
$error = '';

if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    $username = trim($_POST['username'] ?? '');
    $password = $_POST['password'] ?? '';

    if ($username === '' || $password === '') {
        $error = 'Please enter your username and password.';
    } else {
        $stmt = $pdo-&gt;prepare('SELECT id, username, password, role FROM users WHERE username = ?');
        $stmt-&gt;execute([$username]);
        $user = $stmt-&gt;fetch();

        if ($user &amp;&amp; password_verify($password, $user['password'])) {
            $_SESSION['user_id']  = $user['id'];
            $_SESSION['username'] = $user['username'];
            $_SESSION['role']     = $user['role'];
            header('Location: index.php?view=products');
            exit;
        } else {
            // Generic message for security (don't reveal if username or password was wrong)
            $error = 'Invalid username or password.';
        }
    }
}

// Render the view
require 'views/login_view.php';
</code></pre>
<p>7. Last but not least he Login view (<strong><code>views/login_view.php</code>)</strong></p>
<pre><code>
&lt;?php require 'includes/header.php'; ?&gt;

&lt;!-- Display Error Message --&gt;
&lt;?php if (!empty($error)): ?&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;alert alert-danger&quot;&gt;&lt;?= htmlspecialchars($error) ?&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;?php endif; ?&gt;

&lt;form method=&quot;post&quot; style=&quot;max-width:400px;&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;mb-3&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label class=&quot;form-label&quot;&gt;Username&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;username&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; value=&quot;&lt;?= htmlspecialchars($username ?? '') ?&gt;&quot; required&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;mb-3&quot;&gt;
        &lt;label class=&quot;form-label&quot;&gt;Password&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; required&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-primary&quot;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;mt-3&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Don't have an account? &lt;a href=&quot;index.php?view=register&quot;&gt;Register here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;?php require 'includes/footer.php'; ?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>8. The Logout (controllers/logout_controller.php)</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
session_unset();
session_destroy();
header('Location: index.php?view=login');
exit;
</code></pre>
<p>Hope this helps you!</p>
